
- pnpm = performant npm
- keeps one global copy of dependencies for all projects on the disk. It saves space and time because we don't have to install them agian.
- pnpm also has builtin support for monorepos via workspaces
"- packages:
  - "packages/*"
  these lines above under pnpm yaml files tells pnpm that every folder inside packages is its own package.
- pnpm solves dependency linking
- Nx solves building orchestration. Makes a graph to schedule and cache tasks
- Quick way to hold it:

Linking = "can this import find its target?" → package manager's job (pnpm).
Orchestration = "given they're linked, in what order do I build, and what can I skip or cache?" → Nx's job.

- Prisma's DX and migration tooling are more polished, but it hides SQL behind an engine and bloats the container. You chose SQL-proximity and lean images on purpose.

What NestJS even is (30 seconds)
NestJS is a backend framework that imposes structure on a Node API — modules, classes, and a dependency-injection system — instead of the free-for-all of raw Express. It's heavily inspired by Angular's architecture. The tradeoff: more boilerplate and concepts up front, in exchange for a codebase that stays organized as it grows and is testable by design. That "testable by design" part is the senior payoff, and it comes almost entirely from DI, below.

Alternative rejected: plain Express/Fastify. Less to learn, but you hand-wire everything and testing means manually stubbing dependencies. 

A module is a box that groups related code for one domain — controllers, services, and providers that belong together.
Why it matters: boundaries. ClaimsModule shouldn't reach into policy internals — it goes through PoliciesModule's exported surface. This keeps a 30-file codebase from becoming a mud ball where everything imports everything.

Concept 2: Dependency Injection (the important one)
This is the concept interviewers probe, so slow down here.
The problem it solves. A ClaimsService needs a database connection. The naive way: the service creates its own connection inside itself (new Database(...)). Now that service is welded to a real database — you can't test it without a real DB, and you can't swap the DB for a fake. Its dependency is hardcoded.
Dependency injection flips this: instead of a class creating its dependencies, they're handed to it from outside — through its constructor. The class just declares "I need a database" and Nest supplies it:
ts@Injectable()
export class ClaimsService {
  constructor(private readonly db: DatabaseService) {}
  //          ^ Nest sees this, finds the DatabaseService provider,
  //            constructs it once, and injects it here automatically.
}

Provider — anything Nest can inject (usually a service). You register it in a module's providers.
@Injectable() — decorator marking a class as injectable.
Injection token — the key Nest uses to look up what to inject. Usually the class itself; sometimes a string (you'll see this in a second with Drizzle).

Drizzle isn't a Nest-native library — it's just a function that returns a db client. To make it injectable everywhere via DI, you wrap it in a provider once, in one module, and export it.
Every other module imports DatabaseModule and injects the db. One place owns the connection; nothing else calls drizzle() directly.

Supabase Auth is a hosted service that manages signup/login and issues a JWT (JSON Web Token) on successful login. A JWT is a signed token the client sends with every request — signed meaning your backend can verify it wasn't tampered with using a secret, without calling Supabase on every request. The token carries the user's id and metadata in its payload.
A guard in Nest is a class that runs before your route handler and returns true (allow) or false (block). It's the gatekeeper. Auth is the classic use: the guard inspects the incoming request, verifies the JWT, and either attaches the user and lets it through, or throws 401 Unauthorized.
Why a guard and not just code inside each controller: it's cross-cutting. Every protected route needs the same check. Putting it in a guard means you write it once and apply it declaratively with @UseGuards() — no copy-pasting auth logic into 20 handlers.

Alternative rejected: middleware. Nest has middleware too, but guards are purpose-built for authorization — they run in Nest's lifecycle after routing, so they know which handler and which roles are involved (via metadata), which plain middleware doesn't cleanly. Guards for "can this request proceed," middleware for "transform/log this request." Interviewer bait: "guard vs middleware?" — that's the distinction.

One honest tradeoff in that code — I used supabase.auth.getUser(token), which makes a network call to Supabase to validate. The faster alternative is verifying the JWT signature locally with SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET (no network hop) using a library like jose. 
Local verification is what you'd do at scale. I chose the network-call version here because it's simpler to get right and impossible to misconfigure — and for your demo's traffic, the latency is irrelevant.

auth.guard.ts = the imperative implementation. Written once. This is the "how."
@UseGuards(AuthGuard) = the declarative application. Written per-route. This is the "what I want" — a label pointing at the how.

@nestjs/config — the package name. The @nestjs/ prefix is a scope — it marks this as an official NestJS package (same family as @nestjs/common, @nestjs/core). Scopes group related packages under one namespace.

What the package does — it's Nest's official configuration module. Its main job is reading your .env file and loading those values into process.env at startup, plus giving you ConfigService to access them through Nest's DI. 

Declarative = you describe what you want, and the framework figures out how to do it. The opposite is imperative = you write out the step-by-step how yourself.


----------- -------------- ---------------
The reason people use it is speed, and that comes from a specific trick. Older tools like webpack bundled your entire app into one big file before the dev server could even start — so as your project grew, startup got slower and slower. Vite skips that. In development it serves your files directly to the browser as native ES modules, letting the browser request what it actually needs. Startup is near-instant regardless of project size, and when you save a file it swaps just that module in place rather than rebuilding.
Concretely, it gives you:

Instant dev server startup
Fast hot module replacement (edits appear without a full page reload)
TypeScript, JSX, and CSS handling out of the box with no config
A production build (via Rollup) that's properly bundled and optimized

Bundling means taking your many source files and combining them into a small number of output files that a browser can efficiently load.

Modern code is written in lots of small modules — you import things from each other, plus from node_modules. A real app might be hundreds or thousands of files once you count dependencies. That's great for you as a developer. It's terrible for a browser on a network, because each file is a separate HTTP request, and requests have overhead. Loading 800 files means 800 round trips.

So a bundler walks your import graph: it starts at your entry point, follows every import, follows their imports, and so on, until it has the complete set of code your app actually needs. Then it concatenates all of it into one file (or a handful), rewriting the imports so everything still wires up correctly.

While it's doing that walk, it also does some useful things it can only do with the whole picture in view:

Tree-shaking — if you import one function from a library but never use the other fifty, they get dropped. The bundler knows they're unreachable.
Minification — strips whitespace, shortens variable names, removes comments. Same behavior, much smaller file.
Asset handling — CSS, images, and fonts get processed and given hashed filenames for caching.
Code splitting — the opposite of naive bundling: instead of one giant file, it can split out chunks that load on demand (say, the admin dashboard only loads when someone visits /admin).
So a project like this:

src/main.ts → imports Button.ts → imports utils.ts
            → imports react (from node_modules)
becomes roughly:

dist/index-a3f9c2.js     (all of it, tree-shaken and minified)
dist/index-b81e04.css
Which ties back to the Vite point: in development, Vite deliberately doesn't bundle. It serves your files unbundled as native ES modules, because the browser is on localhost and round trips are free — so you get instant startup and fast updates. In production, it bundles via Rollup, because now your users are on real networks and the tradeoff flips completely.

That split is the whole insight behind Vite. Same app, two different strategies, each matched to what actually matters in that context.


