Phase 2

Zod validation — the pipe
Concept: a pipe in Nest transforms or validates input before it reaches your handler. Your spec mandates Zod on all API inputs. The reason: @Body() gives you any — TypeScript's types vanish at runtime, so a client can POST { year: "banana" } and TS won't stop it. Zod validates at runtime and infers the type.

**Imp
Intern vs senior: intern trusts @Body() dto: CreatePolicyDto because it's typed — but that type is a compile-time fiction; nothing checks it at runtime. Senior validates at the boundary and treats every external input as hostile until parsed.

safeParse returns a result object rather than throwing — so you control the error shape. Returning result.data (not value) matters: Zod strips unknown keys and applies coercions, so the handler gets clean data.

z.infer derives the TS type from the runtime schema — one definition, both guarantees. That's the whole point of Zod over hand-written DTOs: they can't drift apart.


pnpm = package manager. Installs deps, links workspace packages so import "@claimpilot/shared" resolves. Your pnpm add, pnpm install.

Nx = build orchestrator. Sits on top of pnpm. Knows api depends on shared, so it builds them in the right order, skips unchanged packages, and caches results.

The split you asked about before:

Linking ("can this import find its target?") → pnpm
Orchestration ("what order, what can I skip/cache?") → Nx
Your current friction — manually running pnpm --filter @claimpilot/shared build before the API build — is orchestration. Nx would infer that and do it automatically. It's just not worth its complexity for two commands yet.

The ownership check — and(eq(id), eq(userId)). Without the userId clause, anyone with a policy id could generate a checkout session for someone else's policy. Filtering by owner in the query (not fetching then comparing in JS) is the habit: authorization belongs in the WHERE clause. This class of bug — IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) — is one of the most common real-world vulnerabilities.


